{#- Managed by infra-hetzner/ansible/roles/app — do not edit by hand.
    Re-rendered on every `ansible-playbook --tags app` run from
    `clickhouse_cn_user_pairs` in group_vars/all.yml.

    Imported into Caddyfile via `import snippets/cn_user_map.snippet`.
    Replaces the earlier `cn_allowlist.snippet` (task 0240): the map
    IS the allowlist — any cert whose CN is not in the map leaves
    `{ch_user}` empty, and the site block's `@no_user` matcher returns
    403 before any backend hop.

    ## Why we match on full DN, not just CN

    Caddy 2.x does NOT register a `{http.request.tls.client.subject_cn}`
    placeholder. `subject` returns the full RFC-2253 Distinguished Name
    string (pkix.Name.String()), e.g. `CN=alice-laptop` for a cert
    issued by issue-client-cert.sh (which sets Subject to `/CN=<name>`
    with no other RDNs). We therefore match against the `CN=…` form.

    ## Why CN regex is enforced at preflight, not here

    Caddy's `map` directive does not run an expression against the
    key, so a CN like `alice"; DROP TABLE x;--` would silently land
    in the rendered output (causing either a Caddyfile parse error
    or — worse — quietly widening the matched set if Caddy ever
    starts honouring escape sequences). The site.yml preflight
    asserts every CN against `^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$` BEFORE Ansible
    reaches the box; the same regex the issuance script enforces.

    An empty `clickhouse_cn_user_pairs` would emit an empty
    `map { }` block. Caddy parses that fine; the site block's
    `@no_user expression {ch_user} == ""` matcher then rejects
    every request with 403. The site.yml preflight refuses the
    empty case (`length > 0`) so we never reach that state in
    practice — it's just the fail-closed behaviour at the Caddy
    layer if the preflight is ever bypassed. -#}
map {http.request.tls.client.subject} {ch_user} {
{% for pair in clickhouse_cn_user_pairs %}
{%   set cn, user = (pair.split(':', 1) | map('trim') | list) %}
    "CN={{ cn }}"    {{ user }}
{% endfor %}
}
